                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the inpatient treatment of your 62-year-old female patient, admitted on 20/02/1991 with acute lower abdominal pain and suspected tubal pregnancy. On admission, clinical examination, transvaginal ultrasonography, and serum beta-hCG measurement confirmed a right-sided tubal ectopic pregnancy without signs of rupture. Given the patient's age and haemodynamic stability, surgical intervention was planned. On 21/02/1991, she underwent laparoscopic salpingectomy with complete removal of the ectopic pregnancy. The postoperative course was uneventful. Wound care was provided and vital signs remained stable throughout. The patient was mobilised successfully and discharged home on 24/02/1991 in good general condition. Please arrange follow-up including monitoring of beta-hCG levels to confirm complete resolution. I remain available for any further queries.</div>"
            },
